            <ArticleTitle>Recent advances in the understanding and management of bipolar disorder in adults.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2033</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.12688/f1000research.12329.1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This article focuses on some aspects of recent progress in the neurobiology and treatment of bipolar disorder (BD) in adults. A molecular-genetic approach to the etiopathogenesis of the illness resulted in the findings of a genetic overlap between BD and other major psychiatric disorders. Furthermore, a poly-gene-environmental interaction in the development of the illness has been demonstrated. For the management of BD, new drugs with putative mood-stabilizing properties have been introduced in the past two decades. However, none of these can surpass lithium, the prototype mood-stabilizer, still considered the most specific drug for BD. Recent research on lithium, besides providing new data on the neurobiology of BD, has confirmed anti-suicidal, immunomodulatory, and neuroprotective properties of this drug.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
